(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-axial-musculature-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"The Axial Musculature — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 4","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The deep (intrinsic) back muscles function primarily to do what?","choices":["Move the upper limb at the shoulder","Compress the abdominal organs","Extend and stabilize the vertebral column","Rotate the eyeball in the orbit"],"answer":2,"exp":"The deep intrinsic back muscles extend, rotate, and stabilize the vertebral column and maintain posture. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"The superficial (extrinsic) back muscles, such as the trapezius and latissimus dorsi, mainly act on what?","choices":["The upper limb and pectoral girdle","The vertebral column only","The abdominal organs","The muscles of the tongue"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superficial extrinsic back muscles attach to and move the upper limb and pectoral girdle. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which large group of intrinsic back muscles, running vertically in three columns, is the main extensor of the spine?","choices":["The rectus abdominis","The external oblique","The erector spinae","The diaphragm muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The erector spinae, made of the iliocostalis, longissimus, and spinalis columns, is the main extensor of the vertebral column. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"The erector spinae is composed of which three muscle columns from lateral to medial?","choices":["Trapezius, rhomboid, and levator","Genioglossus, hyoglossus, styloglossus","Iliocostalis, longissimus, and spinalis","Masseter, temporalis, and pterygoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"From lateral to medial, the erector spinae comprises the iliocostalis, longissimus, and spinalis columns. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which intercostal muscles elevate the ribs to assist inspiration?","choices":["The external intercostals","The internal intercostals","The transversus abdominis","The levator ani"],"answer":0,"exp":"The external intercostal muscles elevate the ribs during inspiration, increasing the volume of the thoracic cavity. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"The diaphragm is the primary muscle of which vital function?","choices":["Respiration (breathing)","Chewing food","Rotating the head","Flexing the elbow"],"answer":0,"exp":"The diaphragm is the primary muscle of respiration, contracting to draw air into the lungs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"The diaphragm is innervated by which nerve, arising from cervical spinal levels three through five?","choices":["The vagus nerve","The phrenic nerve","The sciatic nerve","The facial nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The phrenic nerve, from cervical levels three through five, supplies the diaphragm. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"The strong central tendon of the diaphragm serves what role?","choices":["It is a bony plate anchoring the ribs","It is the flat tendon where the fibers insert","It is a cartilage pad between vertebrae","It is a fluid-filled joint capsule"],"answer":1,"exp":"The central tendon is the flat aponeurotic sheet at the top of the diaphragm into which its radiating muscle fibers insert. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Through which diaphragmatic opening does the esophagus pass?","choices":["The caval opening","The esophageal hiatus","The aortic hiatus","The obturator foramen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The esophagus passes through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm at about the tenth thoracic level. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"How many paired flat and strap muscles form the anterolateral abdominal wall?","choices":["Two paired muscles","Six paired muscles","Eight paired muscles","Four paired muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Four paired muscles form the anterolateral abdominal wall: the rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which vertical, strap-like abdominal muscle flexes the trunk and shows the 'six-pack' segmentation?","choices":["The transversus abdominis","The external oblique","The rectus abdominis","The internal oblique"],"answer":2,"exp":"The rectus abdominis is the vertical strap muscle that flexes the trunk; its tendinous intersections create the six-pack appearance. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"The right and left rectus abdominis muscles are separated in the midline by which fibrous band?","choices":["The linea alba (white line)","The inguinal ligament","The nuchal ligament","The interosseous membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"The linea alba is the fibrous midline band, formed by the fusing aponeuroses, that separates the two rectus abdominis muscles. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which is the deepest of the flat abdominal muscles, with horizontally running fibers that compress the abdomen?","choices":["The external oblique","The rectus abdominis","The erector spinae","The transversus abdominis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The transversus abdominis is the deepest flat abdominal muscle; its transverse fibers compress and support the abdominal contents. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which is the most superficial of the three flat abdominal muscles, its fibers running downward and medially?","choices":["The internal oblique","The transversus abdominis","The external oblique","The rectus abdominis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The external oblique is the most superficial flat abdominal muscle, with fibers directed inferomedially, like hands in the pockets. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"The scalene muscles of the lateral neck perform which action on the ribs?","choices":["They depress the last two ribs","They elevate the first two ribs","They rotate the eyeball","They close the eyelids"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anterior, middle, and posterior scalenes elevate the first two ribs and laterally flex the neck. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"The paired levator ani and coccygeus muscles together form what structure?","choices":["The thoracic diaphragm","The rotator cuff","The pelvic diaphragm","The rectus sheath"],"answer":2,"exp":"The levator ani and coccygeus together form the pelvic diaphragm, the muscular floor that supports the pelvic organs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"The main function of the pelvic floor (pelvic diaphragm) muscles is to do what?","choices":["Support the pelvic organs","Elevate the ribs in breathing","Rotate the forearm bones","Extend the vertebral column"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm supports the bladder, rectum, and other pelvic organs and resists the downward pressure of the abdomen. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"The perineum is divided into an anterior urogenital triangle and which posterior triangle?","choices":["The femoral triangle","The cervical triangle","The anal triangle","The lumbar triangle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perineum is divided into the anterior urogenital triangle and the posterior anal triangle, which contains the anal opening. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which muscle voluntarily controls the closure of the anal opening?","choices":["The orbicularis oculi","The diaphragm muscle","The masseter muscle","The external anal sphincter"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external anal sphincter is the voluntary skeletal muscle that closes the anal opening. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"The serratus posterior superior and inferior muscles are considered accessory muscles of what?","choices":["Mastication","Eye movement","Swallowing","Respiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"The serratus posterior superior and inferior are thin muscles that assist respiration by acting on the ribs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Besides the abdominal muscles, which deep muscle helps flex the trunk and also flexes the hip?","choices":["The psoas major","The erector spinae","The trapezius muscle","The masseter muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The psoas major, running from the lumbar vertebrae to the femur, flexes the trunk and the hip. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"When the diaphragm contracts, its dome does what to draw air into the lungs?","choices":["It rises and dome-shapes upward","It rotates to one side","It splits into two halves","It flattens and descends"],"answer":3,"exp":"On contraction the diaphragm's dome flattens and descends, enlarging the thoracic cavity and drawing air into the lungs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which deeper group of intrinsic back muscles, lying beneath the erector spinae, includes the multifidus and rotates the spine?","choices":["The extrinsic back group","The abdominal oblique group","The pharyngeal constrictor group","The transversospinalis group"],"answer":3,"exp":"The transversospinalis group, including the semispinalis, multifidus, and rotatores, lies deep to the erector spinae and rotates and stabilizes the spine. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which intercostal muscles depress the ribs during forced expiration?","choices":["The external intercostals","The internal intercostals","The scalene muscles","The levator ani muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"The internal intercostal muscles depress the ribs during forced expiration, decreasing thoracic volume. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"The combined contraction of the abdominal muscles raises intra-abdominal pressure to assist in what?","choices":["Rotating the eyeball outward","Forced expiration and coughing","Elevating the mandible","Flexing the wrist joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Contracting the abdominal wall raises intra-abdominal pressure to assist forced expiration, coughing, defecation, and childbirth. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"When only one sternocleidomastoid contracts, what happens to the head?","choices":["It turns the face toward the opposite side","It pulls the face straight down","It rotates the eyeball in place","It closes the jaw tightly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Contraction of one sternocleidomastoid rotates the face toward the opposite side and tilts the head toward the same side. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The urogenital triangle is the anterior part of which body region?","choices":["The thoracic cavity","The cranial cavity","The axilla","The perineum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The urogenital triangle is the anterior portion of the perineum, containing the external genital and urethral structures. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"The back muscles are broadly divided into which two categories?","choices":["Voluntary and smooth muscles","Superficial and deep muscles","Cardiac and skeletal muscles","Flexor and rotator cuff muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"The back muscles are divided into superficial extrinsic muscles that move the limb and deep intrinsic muscles that move the spine. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"The intercostal muscles occupy the spaces between which structures?","choices":["The ribs","The vertebrae","The tarsal bones","The phalanges"],"answer":0,"exp":"The intercostal muscles fill the intercostal spaces between adjacent ribs and act on them during breathing. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"The levator ani is made of the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and which third part?","choices":["The masseter","The iliococcygeus","The genioglossus","The soleus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The levator ani comprises the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus, which together form most of the pelvic diaphragm. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"}]};
